<td id="S3.T1.2.13.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">67.8<sub id="S3.T1.2.13.3.1" class="ltx_sub"><math id="S3.T1.m67" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math>1.6</sub></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.13.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">90.1<sub id="S3.T1.2.13.4.1" class="ltx_sub"><math id="S3.T1.m68" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math>1.8</sub></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.13.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">94.6<sub id="S3.T1.2.13.5.1" class="ltx_sub"><math id="S3.T1.m69" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math>0.2</sub></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.13.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">87.7<sub id="S3.T1.2.13.6.1" class="ltx_sub"><math id="S3.T1.m70" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math>0.7</sub></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.13.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">91.9<sub id="S3.T1.2.13.7.1" class="ltx_sub"><math id="S3.T1.m71" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math>0.1</sub></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.13.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">96.5<sub id="S3.T1.2.13.8.1" class="ltx_sub"><math id="S3.T1.m72" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math>0.1</sub></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.13.9" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">88.1</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.14" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.2.14.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r"><math id="S3.T1.m73" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Ro}_{\text{Large}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>Ro</mtext><mtext>Large</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Ro}_{\text{Large}}</annotation></semantics></math>(IniLoRA)</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.14.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r">0.8M</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.14.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><span id="S3.T1.2.14.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">69.4<sub id="S3.T1.2.14.3.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><math id="S3.T1.m74" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math>0.6</sub></span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.14.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">90.0<sub id="S3.T1.2.14.4.1" class="ltx_sub"><math id="S3.T1.m75" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math>0.4</sub></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.14.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">94.6<sub id="S3.T1.2.14.5.1" class="ltx_sub"><math id="S3.T1.m76" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math>0.3</sub></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.14.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><span id="S3.T1.2.14.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">88.1<sub id="S3.T1.2.14.6.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><math id="S3.T1.m77" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math>0.9</sub></span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.14.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">92.1<sub id="S3.T1.2.14.7.1" class="ltx_sub"><math id="S3.T1.m78" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math>0.3</sub></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.14.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><span id="S3.T1.2.14.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">97.0<sub id="S3.T1.2.14.8.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><math id="S3.T1.m79" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math>0.1</sub></span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.14.9" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><span id="S3.T1.2.14.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">88.5</span></td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The LoRA method updates pre-trained weights <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times k}</annotation></semantics></math> using low-rank matrices <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times r}</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W=BA" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W=BA</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\ll\min(d,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≪</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\ll\min(d,k)</annotation></semantics></math>. Only <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> are fine-tuned via gradient descent, reducing memory use and speeding up training. IniLoRA, proposed in this paper, follows the same low-rank structure but introduces a novel initialization strategy for <math id="S3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, using matrix decomposition optimized by gradient descent (as shown in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Optimizing Fine-Tuning through Advanced Initialization Strategies for Low-Rank Adaptation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To approximate the weight matrix <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, two low-rank matrices <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{(0)}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{(0)}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{(0)}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{(0)}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times r}</annotation></semantics></math> are initialized using a normal distribution. Rather than deriving the initialization parameters directly from <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, a global approach is adopted for stability: the mean <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and standard deviation <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of each layer’s weight matrix <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> (for <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\dots,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\dots,n</annotation></semantics></math>) are computed, and their averages are used to define a unified initialization distribution for <math id="S3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math>. This leverages the observed similarity in weight distributions across layers to streamline the initialization process.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}=\frac{1}{|W^{i}|}\sum_{w\in W^{i}}w\;\;;\;\;\sigma_{i}=\sqrt{\frac{1}{|W^{i}|}\sum_{w\in W^{i}}(w-\mu_{i})^{2}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></munder><mi>w</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.560em" rspace="0.727em">;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}=\frac{1}{|W^{i}|}\sum_{w\in W^{i}}w\;\;;\;\;\sigma_{i}=\sqrt{\frac{1}{|W^{i}|}\sum_{w\in W^{i}}(w-\mu_{i})^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The average of the means and standard deviations across all layers is then computed as:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mu}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mu_{i}\;\;;\;\;\overline{\sigma}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sigma_{i}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.727em">;</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mu}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mu_{i}\;\;;\;\;\overline{\sigma}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The averaged values <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> serve as global initialization parameters, setting <math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\text{init}}=\overline{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>init</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\text{init}}=\overline{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\text{init}}=\overline{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mtext>init</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\text{init}}=\overline{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> for all layers when initializing matrices <math id="S3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>. A low-rank approximation is then optimized by minimizing the mean squared error between <math id="S3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="BA" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">BA</annotation></semantics></math> and the target weight matrix <math id="S3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, using gradient descent with sufficient iterations, according to the following update rules for <math id="S3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{(t+1)}=A^{(t)}-\alpha\frac{\partial}{\partial A}\left\|W_{0}-BA\right\|_{F}^{2}\;\;;\;\;B^{(t+1)}=B^{(t)}-\alpha\frac{\partial}{\partial B}\left\|W_{0}-BA\right\|_{F}^{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.727em">;</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{(t+1)}=A^{(t)}-\alpha\frac{\partial}{\partial A}\left\|W_{0}-BA\right\|_{F}^{2}\;\;;\;\;B^{(t+1)}=B^{(t)}-\alpha\frac{\partial}{\partial B}\left\|W_{0}-BA\right\|_{F}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is the learning rate, and <math id="S3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> represents the <math id="S3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-th iteration step. After <math id="S3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> iterations, assuming the loss has converged, the final matrices <math id="S3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{(T)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{(T)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{(T)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{(T)}</annotation></semantics></math> are used to initialize the low-rank matrices <math id="S3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> for subsequent fine-tuning. However, even after sufficient iterations, a residual may exist between <math id="S3.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{(T)}A^{(T)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{(T)}A^{(T)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. To address this, we define the residual matrix <math id="S3.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times k}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=W_{0}-B^{(T)}A^{(T)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=W_{0}-B^{(T)}A^{(T)}</annotation></semantics></math>. During fine-tuning, only the matrices <math id="S3.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> are updated, while the residual matrix <math id="S3.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> remains fixed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">IniLoRA exploits differences in weight updates between full parameter fine-tuning and LoRA-based fine-tuning during the initial training phase. IniLoRA ensures that the weight adjustments during the initial training phase closely resemble those obtained through full parameter fine-tuning, as the matrix product <math id="S3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="BA" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">BA</annotation></semantics></math> closely approximates the initial weight matrix <math id="S3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, IniLoRA introduces an additional weight approximation step optimized using gradient descent. For any large foundational model, this weight approximation is performed only once, with the resulting matrices <math id="S3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{(T)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{(T)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{(T)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{(T)}</annotation></semantics></math> being cached. As a result, whenever IniLoRA fine-tuning is performed, the pre-approximated weights are retrieved directly from the local cache, eliminating the need for repeated calculations before each fine-tuning session, noticeably enhancing efficiency.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">When weight approximation iterations are set to zero, IniLoRA relies on randomly initialized low-rank matrices. Unlike LoRA’s zero-initialized product, this leads to a non-zero product. As shown in Table <a href="#A1.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ Appendix A Additional Results ‣ Optimizing Fine-Tuning through Advanced Initialization Strategies for Low-Rank Adaptation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> in Appendix, IniLoRA still achieves competitive results on the GSM8K dataset under this setting with the LLaMA2-7B model, directly challenging LoRA’s initialization strategy. Motivated by this observation, we propose IniLoRA-<math id="S3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, which further improves initialization by adjusting the standard deviation. Specifically, IniLoRA-<math id="S3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> employs a broader initialization distribution for the low-rank matrices <math id="S3.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, using</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A,B\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{\alpha}^{2}),\quad\sigma_{\alpha}&gt;\sigma_{\text{init}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mtext>init</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A,B\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{\alpha}^{2}),\quad\sigma_{\alpha}&gt;\sigma_{\text{init}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p11.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> is set to a relatively larger standard deviation (<math id="S3.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\alpha}=0.5)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\alpha}=0.5)</annotation></semantics></math>, in contrast to the standard IniLoRA initialization with <math id="S3.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\text{init}}=\overline{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mtext>init</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\text{init}}=\overline{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>. Once the low-rank matrices <math id="S3.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> are initialized, fine-tuning can begin directly. This adjustment increases the variance of the initial weight distribution, allowing the low-rank matrices to explore a wider region of the parameter space.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p12.1" class="ltx_p">By contrast, IniLoRA-<math id="S3.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> employs low-rank matrices initialized using the Kaiming distribution, which better aligns with the requirements of weight initialization and gradient propagation in deep networks, stabilizes input–output variance, and demonstrates superior performance compared to both LoRA and IniLoRA.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2. </span>Performance comparison of different PEFT approaches across various tasks.</figcaption>
