<tr id="S4.T3.2.11" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T3.2.11.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">IniLoRA-<math id="S4.T3.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>(K-U)</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.11.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">39.7<sub id="S4.T3.2.11.2.1" class="ltx_sub"><math id="S4.T3.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math>0.4</sub></td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.11.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">42.3<sub id="S4.T3.2.11.3.1" class="ltx_sub"><math id="S4.T3.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math>1.0</sub></td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We trained on MetaMathQA and evaluated on GSM8K, with results summarized in Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.2.3. IniLoRA-𝛼 and IniLoRA-𝛽: Performance on NLG ‣ 4.2. Main Results ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ Optimizing Fine-Tuning through Advanced Initialization Strategies for Low-Rank Adaptation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. IniLoRA-<math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, which employs a higher initialization standard deviation (0.5), delivers notable performance improvements, consistently surpassing both LoRA and IniLoRA. IniLoRA-<math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> enhances low-rank adaptation by initializing both matrices with the same Kaiming distribution—either normal (K-N) or uniform (K-U). Across evaluations, both variants of IniLoRA-<math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> outperform LoRA and IniLoRA, achieving superior results.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3. </span>Analysis</h3>

<section id="S4.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.3.1. </span>Evaluating IniLoRA: Performance, Scalability, and Robustness</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A1.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ Appendix A Additional Results ‣ Optimizing Fine-Tuning through Advanced Initialization Strategies for Low-Rank Adaptation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> in the Appendix shows that IniLoRA consistently surpasses other methods across all ranks on the GSM8K dataset with the LLaMA2-7B model, achieving the best overall performance. Figure <a href="#A1.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Appendix A Additional Results ‣ Optimizing Fine-Tuning through Advanced Initialization Strategies for Low-Rank Adaptation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> in the Appendix further compares the losses of IniLoRA and LoRA under different ranks, demonstrating that IniLoRA consistently maintains lower loss than LoRA.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate scalability, we trained IniLoRA on the MetaMathQA dataset with 10K, 50K, and 100K samples (one epoch) using LLaMA2-7B model, and averaged GSM8K test accuracy over multiple trials. As shown in Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.3.1. Evaluating IniLoRA: Performance, Scalability, and Robustness ‣ 4.3. Analysis ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ Optimizing Fine-Tuning through Advanced Initialization Strategies for Low-Rank Adaptation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, both LoRA and IniLoRA improve with more data, but IniLoRA consistently achieves higher accuracy, confirming its effectiveness—especially with larger datasets and higher ranks. Further work could explore its broader scalability.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4. </span>Performance comparison of LoRA and IniLoRA with different data sizes and ranks.</figcaption>
<table id="S4.T4.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S4.T4.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T4.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T4.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Data Size</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T4.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Method</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T4.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rank 8</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T4.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rank 16</span></td>
